{"name":"Flower Power","hex":"#e6aed3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flower power","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A counterculture movement that started in the United States in the 1960s, advocating peace and love over militarism and materialism."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flower_power"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flower power","blocks":[{"text":"Flower power was a political movement and slogan used during the late 1960s and early 1970s as a symbol of passive resistance and nonviolence. It is rooted in the anti-war movement which was opposed to U.S. involvement in the Vietnam War. The expression was coined by Beat poet Allen Ginsberg in 1965 as a means to transform war protests into peaceful affirmative spectacles. Hippies embraced the symbolism by dressing in clothing with embroidered flowers and vibrant colors, wearing flowers in their hair, and distributing flowers to the public, becoming known as flower children. The term later became generalized as a modern reference to the hippie movement and the so-called counterculture of drugs, psychedelic music, psychedelic art, and social permissiveness."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/A_female_demonstrator_offers_a_flower_to_military_police_on_guard_at_the_Pentagon_during_an_anti-Vietnam_demonstration._Arlington%2C_Virginia%2C_USA.jpg/330px-A_female_demonstrator_offers_a_flower_to_military_police_on_guard_at_the_Pentagon_during_an_anti-Vietnam_demonstration._Arlington%2C_Virginia%2C_USA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower_power","wikidata":"Q1254661"},{"title":"Flower Power (photograph)","blocks":[{"text":"Flower Power is the title of a photograph taken by American photographer Bernie Boston for the now-defunct newspaper The Washington Evening Star. Taken on October 21, 1967, during the March on the Pentagon by the National Mobilization Committee to End the War in Vietnam, the photo shows a protester placing a carnation into the barrel of an M14 rifle held by a soldier of the 503rd Military Police Battalion (Airborne)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/52/Flower_Power_by_Bernie_Boston.jpg/330px-Flower_Power_by_Bernie_Boston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower_Power_(photograph)","wikidata":"Q5462192"},{"title":"Flower","blocks":[{"text":"Flowers, also known as blossoms and blooms, are the reproductive structures of flowering plants. Typically, they are structured in four circular levels around the end of a stalk. These include: sepals, which are modified leaves that support the flower; petals, often designed to attract pollinators; male stamens, where pollen is presented; and female gynoecia, where pollen is received and its movement is facilitated to the egg. When flowers are arranged in a group, they are known collectively as an inflorescence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg/330px-Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower","wikidata":"Q506"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Late_1960s_cotton_print_fabric.jpg/500px-Late_1960s_cotton_print_fabric.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Late_1960s_cotton_print_fabric.jpg/1280px-Late_1960s_cotton_print_fabric.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Late_1960s_cotton_print_fabric.jpg/500px-Late_1960s_cotton_print_fabric.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cotton fabric, late 1960s, U.S.","link"